# stdlib/physics/mechanics.fw — Newtonian mechanics formulas.
#
# Each formula is a plain bidirectional equation, so any single unknown
# resolves via the standard solver (force=?, mass=?, accel=? all work
# off the same line).
#
# This file inlines the SI-base bindings it depends on so that the
# numeric unit-suffix forms (`mass=10kg`, `accel=9.81m/s^2`) collapse
# end-to-end without a separate `stdlib/units/si-minimal.fw` load.
# The values mirror `stdlib/units/si-minimal.fw`; if that catalog
# changes its base policy, update this file in lockstep.
#
# Variable-naming rule (Units arc cycle 3): SI base-unit symbols
# `m, kg, s, A, K, mol, cd` are RESERVED as scalars by the units
# catalog. Physics quantities use verbose names (`mass`, `displacement`,
# `area`) instead of single-letter shadows. `a`, `v`, `F` are fine
# because they are not SI base-unit symbols.

# --- Inlined SI base + derived units (mirrors stdlib/units/si-minimal.fw) ---
m  = 1
kg = 1
s  = 1
N  = kg * m / s^2
Pa = N / m^2
J  = N * m
W  = J / s

# --- Newton's second law. ---
force = mass * accel

# --- Earth-surface gravitational acceleration and weight. ---
gravity = 981 / 100
weight = mass * gravity

# --- Linear momentum. ---
momentum = mass * velocity

# --- Kinetic energy. ---
kinetic_energy = (mass * velocity^2) / 2

# --- Mechanical work (constant force along straight-line displacement). ---
work = force * displacement

# --- Power. ---
power = work / time

# --- Pressure. ---
pressure = force / area

# --- Frequency-period reciprocity (both directions covered by the solver). ---
frequency = 1 / period
